`,s.jsx(Q,{items:[{q:"Recite LIME's four steps and name the purpose of each.",a:"Perturb — build a sample of inputs around x. Query — get the black box's outputs on them, which is the only access needed. Weight by proximity — make near samples count most, which is what makes the explanation local. Fit a sparse linear model — its coefficients are the human-readable explanation."},{q:"Read the objective L(f, g, pi_x) + Omega(g) out loud in plain English.",a:"Find the simplest model g that still mimics the black box f in the neighbourhood of x. The first term punishes disagreement with f on proximity-weighted samples; the second punishes using too many features."},{q:"What breaks if you remove the proximity kernel, and what breaks if you remove Omega(g)?",a:"Without the kernel, distant samples count equally, so g approximates f over a wide region where f is genuinely nonlinear — the explanation stops being local and becomes a poor global compromise. Without Omega, g spreads tiny weights over every feature: a tighter fit that no human can read, which defeats the purpose."},{q:"Why does LIME fit on a binary 'interpretable representation' rather than the raw input?",a:"Because a coefficient on raw pixel 4,217 or embedding dimension 300 means nothing to a person. Recoding to 'is this superpixel present, is this word present, is this feature near its original value' makes each coefficient read directly as 'this region/word/feature mattered this much'."}]}),`
